<!-- Teacher-story narration source.
     One <speak> block per slide.
     Render via scripts/render_narration.sh (operator workflow).
     Voice: Polly Joanna (neural). -->

<!-- slide-01.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Watch a Grade eleven Physics lesson assemble itself.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Text. <break time="200ms"/> Diagrams. <break time="200ms"/> An animation. <break time="200ms"/> A quiz.
    <break time="500ms"/>
    All scoped to one teacher's curriculum, in real time.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-02.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Linda teaches Grade eleven Science at MilfordWaterford.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    She wants Newton's laws — for her class, anchored to this week's news.
    <break time="500ms"/>
    She types her request and presses generate.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-03.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    The system scopes the query against six dimensions —
    <break time="200ms"/>
    topic, <break time="120ms"/> grade, <break time="120ms"/> language,
    <break time="120ms"/> curriculum context, <break time="120ms"/> format,
    <break time="120ms"/> and the real-world framing.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    That tuple makes the lesson hers — not generic.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-04.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Text first.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    A clear overview. Three learning objectives.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    Key concepts in plain language — pitched two grades below her students for accessibility.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-05.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Then images.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Force-body diagrams. <break time="200ms"/> A motion graph.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    And a hurricane evacuation map drawn from this week's news —
    so the lesson connects to a world Linda's class is already watching.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-06.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    For motion topics, the lesson embeds a short animation.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The animation lives in the lesson — not behind a link, not in a separate tab.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Same engine that builds the lesson, builds the clip.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-07.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Then a quiz.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eight questions. Multiple choice and short answer.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Wrong answers don't get a red X — they get a two-sentence explanation
    written for the misconception, not the topic.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-08.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    For the students who get stuck — a tutorial section.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Step by step. Algebra worked out. Common mistakes called out by name.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Linda doesn't have to write any of it. The lesson does.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-09.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    One engine — every audience.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    The same Newton's laws topic regenerates for Grade twelve Science,
    Grade eleven Commerce, Grade eight STEM —
    each scoped to its own curriculum, its own reading level, its own framing.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-10.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    On Linda's reports page, students whose first-attempt pass rate slips
    get flagged automatically.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    No spreadsheet. No guessing.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    She clicks one row to see the trend, the attempts, and the topics.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-11.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Accessibility is one toggle.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Wider letter spacing. Broader line height. OpenDyslexic mode at the click of an eye-icon.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The setting follows the student across every page, every device.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-12.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    StudyBuddy.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Lessons, always current.
    <break time="600ms"/>
    Visit demo dot studybuddy dot app to see it for yourself —
    or email support at studybuddy dot app to talk.
  </prosody>
</speak>
